From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
Subject: [PATCH] sparc32: enter the prompt on op_enter and resume the client
 with go

The romvec's op_enter (pv_abort) is how a running client asks for the
PROM prompt: Solaris and SunOS call it from the keyboard abort sequence
(Stop-A, L1-A), Solaris also on a break on its serial console, through
prom_enter_mon(). On a Sun the machine stops at "ok" and "go" returns to
the client.

obp_abort() printed "abort, power off" and wrote the power-off bit
instead, so Stop-A on a running Solaris turned the machine off (QEMU:
a guest shutdown request; the guest loses everything not on disk).

Run the Forth interpreter instead, on its own input buffer, until "go",
then return to the client. The client's interrupts stay masked meanwhile
(PIL 15): the console is polled, and the client's own keyboard and serial
handlers would take the keys. While the client is entered, "boot" resets
the machine (reset-all) first, as the Sun PROMs do, since the client
still occupies memory. Stop-A at the firmware's own prompt, with no
client, stays ignored.
---
diff --git a/arch/sparc32/init.fs b/arch/sparc32/init.fs
index 5e8805b..325cc1c 100644
--- a/arch/sparc32/init.fs
+++ b/arch/sparc32/init.fs
@@ -37,6 +37,45 @@
 
 device-end
 
+\ The prompt a running client enters through the romvec (op_enter, see
+\ obp_abort in romvec.c): Stop-A, a break on ttya. "go" returns to the
+\ client; "boot" resets the machine first, since the client is in memory.
+0 value romvec-entered?
+create romvec-enter-ib 100 allot
+
+: (enter) ( -- )
+  save-source
+  romvec-enter-ib ['] ib (to)
+  100 #ib !
+  0 ['] source-id (to)
+  0 >in !
+  0 span !
+  state @ >r 0 state !
+  true to romvec-entered?
+  0 to terminate?
+  cr ." Type  'go' to resume" cr
+  outer-interpreter
+  0 to terminate?
+  false to romvec-entered?
+  r> state !
+  restore-source
+;
+
+: go ( -- )
+  romvec-entered? if 1 to terminate? exit then
+  go
+;
+
+: boot ( "{param-text}<cr>" -- )
+  romvec-entered? if
+    linefeed parse 2drop
+    ." Resetting ..." cr
+    reset-all
+    exit
+  then
+  boot
+;
+
 : rmap@    ( virt -- rmentry )
   drop 0
   ;
diff --git a/arch/sparc32/romvec.c b/arch/sparc32/romvec.c
index 7a01035..760fbf9 100644
--- a/arch/sparc32/romvec.c
+++ b/arch/sparc32/romvec.c
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 #include "libopenbios/sys_info.h"
 #include "boot.h"
 #include "romvec.h"
+#include "psr.h"
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_OBP
 #define DPRINTF(fmt, args...)                   \
@@ -257,12 +258,22 @@ void obp_reboot(char *str)
     for (;;) {}
 }
 
+/*
+ * op_enter: the client asks for the prompt (Solaris and SunOS on Stop-A, or
+ * Solaris on a break on its serial console). The client stays
+ * in memory: run the Forth interpreter until "go", then return to it, as the
+ * Sun PROMs do. Its interrupts stay masked meanwhile: the console is polled,
+ * and the client's own handlers would take the keys.
+ */
 void obp_abort(void)
 {
-    printk("abort, power off\n");
-    *power_reg = 1;
-    printk("power off failed\n");
-    for (;;) {}
+    int dstacktmp = dstackcnt;
+    unsigned int psr = get_psr();
+
+    put_psr(psr | PSR_PIL);
+    fword("(enter)");
+    put_psr((get_psr() & ~PSR_PIL) | (psr & PSR_PIL));
+    dstackcnt = dstacktmp;
 }
 
 void obp_halt(void)
